## Why Pith says this

Highlight any text to comment on it.

Editor's note read the letter

The core new results are a Schur-positive version of the Ahlswede-Daykin inequality that also contains the Lam-Postnikov-Pylyavskyy inequality as a special case, plus a resolution of Mihalcea's conjecture on log-supermodularity for stable Grothendieck polynomials. The abstract frames both cleanly as direct extensions, and the paper lists further generalizations and applications.

What stands out is the explicit containment of the two earlier inequalities; that is a standard and useful way to measure strength in this corner of algebraic combinatorics. If the proofs in the full manuscript are complete and check out, the conjecture resolution is the kind of concrete advance that moves the literature forward.

The main limitation right now is that only the abstract is in front of us, so the actual argument for why Schur positivity is enough to carry the inequality is not visible. No circular definitions or hidden parameters appear in the claims, but without the details it is impossible to judge whether the generalization holds under exactly the stated conditions or needs extra restrictions. The citation pattern looks standard for the area.

This is for readers already working on positivity questions for symmetric functions or Grothendieck polynomials. Someone outside that niche will not get much from it. Inside the niche, the conjecture resolution makes it worth a careful look.

I would send it to peer review. The conjecture resolution alone is enough to justify referee time, even if the proofs need tightening.

What would settle it

A concrete Schur-positive function on a poset that violates the ADS inequality would disprove the generalization.

Referee report

Significance

If the central claims hold, the work would meaningfully extend classical correlation inequalities into the setting of Schur positivity, offering a unified framework that recovers known results as special cases while settling an open conjecture. The combinatorial approach could facilitate new applications in algebraic combinatorics and symmetric function theory.

Minor objections (2)

  1. The abstract states the generalization holds under the conditions of the classical ADS inequality, but the manuscript should explicitly restate those conditions in §1 or §2 to make the scope of the Schur-positive extension fully transparent without requiring the reader to consult the 1978 reference.
  2. Notation for the stable Grothendieck polynomials and the log-supermodularity statement in the resolution of Mihalcea's conjecture should be introduced with a brief self-contained definition in the section where the conjecture is addressed, even if standard in the literature.

We thank the referee for the positive summary, significance assessment, and recommendation of minor revision. The report correctly identifies the main contributions: the Schur-positive generalization of the Ahlswede--Daykin inequality (containing the Lam--Postnikov--Pylyavskyy inequality as a special case) and the resolution of Mihalcea's conjecture on log-supermodularity of stable Grothendieck polynomials.

